    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d260">

                <pb facs="0477" n="431" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg431"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/570½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-07-03">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">3 July, 1917</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: As I have just indicated
                    to you, over the telephone, I entirely approve of this. I hope that you will
                    re-read the latter portion of it, however, with a view to making the idea of
                    Japan’s <hi rendition="#underline">political</hi> influence over China a little
                    more prominent as the thing we have <hi rendition="#underline">not</hi> assented
                    to in the sense she evidently has in mind.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Woodrow Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
